courses.learning_outcomes:
By successfully completing this course, you should be able to

- Acquire a knowledge and understanding of the Chinese political history and contemporary politics of China

- Explore the main theoretical, empirical, and methodological approaches to the study of Chinese politics;

- Relate these approaches to the broader field of comparative politics;

- Develop conceptual ability to analyze and explain major issues and challenges confronting China today.

The course will

* help students grasp the nature of SNS with an overview of its influence in public affairs, especially to policy makers and influencers;
* enhance students' understanding of the new era of news media under the influence of SNS as a dominating form of medium;
* encourage students to academically discuss the analytical framework and real-life applications of social listening as a new means to understand “public opinion”; and
* enable students to, in the shoes of a Public Relations practitioner, use SNS to their advantage in fulfilling different objectives and goals through sharing of industry experience.

By successfully completing this course, you should be able to
 Understand the ways in which the conditions under which democratic regimes are more or less likely to emerge and survive;
 Achieve a broader knowledge about how and to what degree democratic principles are embodied in political practice;
 Think critically about the origins, evolution, maintenance, quality of democracy;
 Analyze cases comparatively by using key concepts and major methods introduced in this course;
 Explain variation in the performance of different types of political regimes;
 Present evidence and construct good arguments from a political science perspective.

By successfully completing this course, you should be able to

 Achieve a broader knowledge about how politics works in a variety of countries.
 Analyze cases comparatively by using key concepts and major methods in comparative politics;
 Give an account of, and critically comment on, debates concerning major issues in today’s world;
 Explain variation (or lack of variation) in political behavior, institutions and outcomes across the global.

Knowledge Outcomes
1. Good knowledge of the fundamental concepts, major theories and intellectual perspectives of public budgeting and finance;
2. Ability to perform basic analysis using fiscal and financial concepts and techniques;
3. Provide a good foundation for students for more advanced training and education in the field;
4. understanding of the major literature and classics of the field;
5. Understanding of the major management and policy implications of issues related to public finance and budgeting.

Skills Outcomes
1. Ability to think critically and analytically;
2. Ability to apply a multidisciplinary and multi-perspective framework in their thinking and analysis;
3. Ability to relate their academic learning to life experience and public issues;
4. Ability to apply relevant concepts and theories of public budgeting and finance to analyze and resolve real-world problems;
5. Develop cross-sector professional and managerial skills including communicating ideas and analysis findings effectively in both verbal and written formats and interpersonal skills;
6. Ability to work independently as well as in a team and collaborated setting;
7. Ability to integrate different fields of knowledge for problem-solving.

Value Outcomes
1. Awareness of the importance and different aspects of knowledge in public budgeting and finance;
2. Awareness of the relevancy and importance of politics and values in public budgeting and finance issues'
3. Appreciation of the use of different perspectives and multiple theories in the analysis of issues and problems of public budgeting and finance;
3. Sense of civil responsibility and engagement in modern society.

